      "sequence": 117,
      "meaning": "Having recognized Bharadvāja’s virtues and character, the sage begins Raghupati’s play. He says today’s meeting has given his mind indescribable joy. Rāma’s deeds are immeasurable; even a hundred million Śeṣas could not tell them. Still, remembering the bow-bearing Lord and master of speech, he will relate what he has heard. Speech is like a wooden dancing doll, its indwelling master Rāma the puppeteer; when he graciously recognizes someone as his own, he makes speech dance in that poet’s heart-courtyard. Bowing to merciful Raghunātha, the sage begins his pure praise. He first describes lovely Mount Kailāsa, where Śiva and Umā always dwell and where siddhas, ascetics, yogīs, heroes, kinnaras and hosts of meritorious sages serve Śiva, source of joy.",
